	<text xml:lang="en">
		<body>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><p>Microsatellite genotyping can be used to identify individuals and thus count or track animals. For endangered birds, individual identification studies are rare because of difficulties associated with capturing them and obtaining samples <ref type="bibr" target="#b11">(Rudnick et al. 2005)</ref>. Recently, many studies have demonstrated that molted feathers can provide an easily available source of DNA in avian studies <ref type="bibr" target="#b2">(Horva ˆth et al. 2005;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b5">Isabel Min ˜o and Nassif Del Lama 2009)</ref>. However, low-quality DNA in molted feathers may lead to low amplification success rates in polymerase chain reaction (PCR) or failure of locus amplification in microsatellite genotyping <ref type="bibr" target="#b7">(Johansson et al. 2012)</ref>. In this study, a novel multiplex PCR assay, based on highly polymorphic microsatellite markers, was developed for individual identification of molted feathers in the vulnerable Chinese Egret (Egretta eulophotes) (IUCN 2014).</p><p>Sampling, storage and DNA isolation of molted feathers and plucked nestlings' feathers were described in the supplementary material. The DNA samples were amplified using species-specific ND6 and tRNA Glu gene primers for species identification <ref type="bibr" target="#b4">(Huang et al. 2012</ref>) and using Chromo-Helicase-DNA binding protein gene (CHD) primers for sex determination <ref type="bibr" target="#b14">(Wang et al. 2011)</ref>. Then, 13 microsatellite loci isolated from the Chinese Egret <ref type="bibr" target="#b3">(Huang et al. 2010;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b0">Dai et al. 2013</ref>) and three markers obtained by cross-amplification from Reddish Egret (Egretta rufescens) <ref type="bibr" target="#b1">(Hill and Green 2011)</ref> were chosen and allocated into four multiplex PCR panels using software MPprimer <ref type="bibr" target="#b13">(Shen et al. 2010)</ref>, based on size limitations and annealing temperature of primers. The 5 0 end of each forward primer was labeled with different fluorescent dyes (HEX, TAMRA or FAM), and the Multiplex PCR panels were performed using TaKaRa Multiplex PCR Assay Kit in 20 lL reaction volume containing 10 lL of Multiplex PCR Mix 2, standardly diluted primer mix, 0.1 lL of Multiplex PCR Mix 1, The polymorphic information of 16 microsatellite loci was evaluated using 134 plucked nestlings' feathers of E. eulophotes (one per nest). Using MICRO-CHECKER <ref type="bibr" target="#b9">(Oosterhout et al. 2004)</ref>, no null allele, large allele drop out or stuttering was found <ref type="bibr" target="#b7">(Johansson et al. 2012</ref>). The GENEPOP program <ref type="bibr" target="#b10">(Rousset 2008)</ref> indicated that all loci conformed to Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium (HWE) after Bonferroni correction except Ae52 which had high polymorphism. The software CERVUS <ref type="bibr" target="#b8">(Kalinowski et al. 2007)</ref> showed that the number of alleles per polymorphic locus ranged between 5 and 32 and observed heterozygosity and expected heterozygosity ranged from 0.552 to 0.955 and 0.633 to 0.955, respectively. The polymorphism information content (PIC) values ranged from 0.586 to 0.949 (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">1</ref>). The exclusion probability of each locus for unrelated individuals (P ID ) and for individuals with sibling relationship (P ID(sib) ) were sorted in ascending order, and the combined exclusion probability were 7.02 9 10 -19 for P ID and 2.9 9 10 -7 for P ID(sib) . The P ID(sib) value of any combination of 7 loci among the 16 markers was high enough ([0.99) for individual identification (Fig. <ref type="figure">1</ref>). Accordingly, CERVUS software was used for identifying individuals with the following criteria: (1) samples were amplified by C7 loci and B1 mismatch occurred; (2) samples from a single individual should be identified as identical species and sex, and belong to the same colony in a given year.</p><p>In species identification, 61 (75.31 %) of 81 molted feathers were identified as E. eulophotes and the remaining 20 (24.69 %) were identified as Little Egret (Egretta garzetta) (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">S1</ref>, Supplementary material). By sex determination and individual identification, 59 of 61 feather samples of E. eulophotes and 18 of 20 feather samples of E. garzetta, being successfully amplified by more than 7 loci, were unambiguously assigned to 43 different individuals (32 females and 11 males) and 13 different individuals (9 females and 4 males), respectively (Table <ref type="table">S2</ref> and Table <ref type="table">S3</ref>, Supplementary material). The success rates of molted feathers amplified by C7 markers was 96.72 % in the current study, in contrast to 47 % of <ref type="bibr" target="#b12">Segelbacher (2002)</ref>, and the mean amplification success rate of 16 markers was 94.47 % (86.89-98.36 %, Table <ref type="table">S4</ref>, Supplementary material), which was higher than 33.68 % (15.4-67.7 %)of 17 loci reported by <ref type="bibr" target="#b7">Johansson et al. (2012)</ref>. These results indicated that our novel multiplex PCR assay was useful for individual identification of molted feathers in E. eulophotes and other Ardeids.</p></div><figure xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head></head><label></label><figDesc></figDesc><graphic coords="3,53.87,59.25,232.44,313.02" type="bitmap" /></figure>
<figure xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" type="table" xml:id="tab_0"><head>Table 1</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Sixteen microsatellite loci arranged into four multiplex PCR panels for the Chinese Egret (Egretta eulophotes)</figDesc><table><row><cell>T multi (°C) Fluorescent dyes Size range (bp) k N Ho He PIC HWE</cell><cell>62 FAM 94-114 6 134 0.791 0.816 0.785 0.283</cell><cell></cell><cell>TAMRA 142-162 6 134 0.552 0.646 0.580 0.049</cell><cell></cell><cell>HEX 118-139 8 134 0.694 0.693 0.641 0.521</cell><cell></cell><cell>FAM 172-200 8 133 0.586 0.746 0.720 0.009</cell><cell></cell><cell>62 FAM 106-122 5 133 0.684 0.634 0.562 0.431</cell><cell></cell><cell>TAMRA 165-213 8 134 0.612 0.636 0.600 0.034</cell><cell></cell><cell>FAM 210-262 12 134 0.858 0.823 0.797 0.013</cell><cell></cell><cell>HEX 125-165 8 134 0.716 0.697 0.654 0.462</cell><cell></cell><cell>56 FAM 117-205 17 133 0.812 0.852 0.831 0.076</cell><cell></cell><cell>TAMRA 177-201 7 132 0.879 0.813 0.782 0.005</cell><cell></cell><cell>FAM 193-438 32 133 0.955 0.955 0.949 *</cell><cell></cell><cell>HEX 133-183 10 132 0.788 0.834 0.811 0.344</cell><cell></cell><cell>56 FAM 110-146 7 134 0.597 0.633 0.562 0.380</cell><cell></cell><cell>TAMRA 123-208 15 134 0.933 0.906 0.895 0.750</cell><cell></cell><cell>FAM 210-238 8 134 0.687 0.695 0.641 0.051</cell><cell></cell><cell>HEX 171-216 10 134 0.843 0.818 0.790 0.037</cell></row><row><cell>Multiplex panel Locus GenBank accession Primer sequence (5 0 -3 0 )</cell><cell>I Ae36 FJ900150 F:TGTCTGAGTAAACAGGCAGTTGGGT</cell><cell>R:TCCCCCTGCAGAAGGATGTGGT</cell><cell>Ae01 FJ900138 F:TGAGCTCTTGCTGGCATCCACCT</cell><cell>R:ACACTTCTCCACCAGCAGCTTTGT</cell><cell>Ee28 KJ748387 F:CGCCAGCTTGGTTAATGCTACAAG</cell><cell>R:CAGAGCACCAGACAGGAAGAGAG</cell><cell>Ee46 KJ748397 F:GAACAGAGAGACTTCCAAACCCTG</cell><cell>R:TGAGGCTCTGGTGATGTTGGTG</cell><cell>II Ae44 FJ900154 F:AGGGAGTCATGGTGGGATAATTCACA</cell><cell>R:CATCTTCCTGGCCTGTCCTTCCT</cell><cell>Ae37 FJ900151 F:CCAGTACAACTCCACGGAGAG